 },
 "literal": "If then [there] is some end of things done which for its own sake we wish, and the other things for the sake of this, and not all things for the sake of another we choose (for it will go on thus at any rate to infinity, so that empty and vain [is] the desire), [it is] clear that this would be the good and the best. Then also for the life the knowledge of it great has weight, and just as archers a target having, more would we hit the needful? And if so, [we] must try in outline at least to grasp it, what ever [it] is, and of which of the sciences or capacities [it is]. And [it] would seem [to be] of the most authoritative and most architectonic. And such the political [science] appears; for which [it is] necessary to be of the sciences in the cities, and what sort each [class is] to learn and up to what [point], this arranges; and we see also the most honoured of the capacities under this being, such as strategy, economics, rhetoric; and this using the remaining [practical] of the sciences, and further legislating what one must do and from what to abstain, the of-this end would include those of the others, so that this would be the human good. For even if the same [it] is for one and for a city, greater at any rate and more complete that of the city appears both to take and to preserve; for [it is] welcome even for one alone, but finer and more divine for a nation and for cities. So the inquiry aims at these, being a sort of political [science]."
}